WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.677
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.677 --> 00:00:07.149
&gt;&gt; Hello, and
welcome to our conference call,

00:00:07.149 --> 00:00:12.110
MBA Admissions Insights: Preparing for
Your Letters of Reference and Essays.

00:00:12.110 --> 00:00:13.750
Thank you for joining us.

00:00:13.750 --> 00:00:15.220
My name is Allison Davis.

00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:18.530
I'm Associate Director in
the MBA Admissions Office.

00:00:18.530 --> 00:00:22.510
I'm joined by Derrick Bolton,
Assistant Dean for MBA Admissions.

00:00:22.510 --> 00:00:23.080
&gt;&gt; Hey, everybody.

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:25.480
&gt;&gt; In the next 30 minutes,

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:29.640
we'll be talking about what to keep in
mind as you prepare your essays and

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:32.720
approach recommenders for
your letters of reference.

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:36.180
We'll start by briefly reviewing
the requirements for both, and

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:38.610
offer some tips along the way.

00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:43.650
We'll also discuss some questions that
you may find useful to ask yourself

00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:48.600
at this stage in the process and
how you might go about answering them.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:52.760
We'll wrap up by answering some of
the questions submitted by you in advance.

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:58.170
Let's start with essay requirements and
tips.

00:00:58.170 --> 00:01:02.570
The requirements, word length,
formatting, etc., for the essay

00:01:02.570 --> 00:01:06.890
questions are all on our website and
you should definitely read all of them.

00:01:06.890 --> 00:01:11.940
But it could be useful to
review the basics again here.

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:17.360
There are two personal essays, essay one
is what matters most to you and why.

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:21.716
This is a pretty fundamental question
that really invites self-examination and

00:01:21.716 --> 00:01:22.885
self-reflection.

00:01:22.885 --> 00:01:27.110
Derrick, what sort of things
are we looking for there?

00:01:27.110 --> 00:01:32.640
&gt;&gt; I think when you said personal essays,
you said more than you even intended to,

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:36.330
because they're not intended
to be professional and

00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:37.850
that you're speaking about work topics.

00:01:37.850 --> 00:01:42.090
But truly a personal
reflection on the people,

00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:46.280
on the experiences, on the situations
that have influenced you.

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:49.430
And more important how those
have shaped your perspectives,

00:01:49.430 --> 00:01:53.195
not just about what your accomplishments
are, what you've achieved.

00:01:53.195 --> 00:01:56.510
We'd say focus on why,
rather than focusing on what.

00:01:58.050 --> 00:02:02.820
&gt;&gt; Second essay question is pretty broad,
it's why Stanford, and

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:08.340
how will earning your MBA at Stanford
enable you to realize your ambitions?

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:10.110
What are we looking for there, Derrick?

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:14.876
&gt;&gt; I think if you look back over
the decade, 15 years that I've been in

00:02:14.876 --> 00:02:19.231
this role, we used to ask a question
that was really specific,

00:02:19.231 --> 00:02:25.370
tell us about your short-term goals,
tell us about your long-term goals.

00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:28.840
Why would an MBA help you and then why
do you want the MBA from Stanford?

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:33.450
Over time,
we've shifted toward a broader question

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:37.760
because we didn't want to lock
applicants in unnecessarily.

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:41.732
It wasn't something we were using in
assessing viability as candidates.

00:02:41.732 --> 00:02:45.730
So we said, what's a question that
will actually help the candidate?

00:02:46.850 --> 00:02:51.350
We want you to reflect on why you want
this graduate education in management,

00:02:51.350 --> 00:02:54.550
how it's going to help you in
realizing your objectives, and

00:02:54.550 --> 00:02:57.940
really thinking about the distinctive
opportunities you'll pursue at Stanford.

00:02:57.940 --> 00:03:01.800
Because once you get here, you get I
think overwhelmed by the sheer number of

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:05.458
choices in front of you, so it's
helpful to do some reflection upfront.

00:03:05.458 --> 00:03:08.800
&gt;&gt; I think I'd even heard you say
sometimes students have actually

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:13.670
held on to their essays and posted them
on their mirror and that kind of thing.

00:03:13.670 --> 00:03:15.740
&gt;&gt; Put them on the door
of your room at Schwab or

00:03:15.740 --> 00:03:19.520
at Highland Hall and everyday,
it's a reminder of why you're here and

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:21.140
what you want to take
away from the experience.

00:03:21.140 --> 00:03:24.960
But I think that gets back to the point,
again, that they're personal essays.

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:29.460
They're meant to be valuable to
the applicant who is writing them.

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:30.690
There are no shortcuts here.

00:03:31.710 --> 00:03:35.307
So you really have to think carefully
about who you are, about the experiences

00:03:35.307 --> 00:03:38.210
that have shaped you, and
about where you want to go from here.

00:03:39.370 --> 00:03:44.150
&gt;&gt; In general, I think our advice is
keep it simple, don't over think it.

00:03:44.150 --> 00:03:47.050
Be sure to actually answer
the questions we're asking,

00:03:47.050 --> 00:03:49.460
follow directions, that sort of thing.

00:03:49.460 --> 00:03:50.120
What else?

00:03:51.530 --> 00:03:54.395
&gt;&gt; We're talking about essays and
letters of reference today and

00:03:54.395 --> 00:03:57.120
at their simplest form,
both of those are stories.

00:03:58.430 --> 00:04:01.910
I think what you're trying to do with
the essays is to tell a story that

00:04:01.910 --> 00:04:02.800
only you can tell.

00:04:03.830 --> 00:04:06.210
It should be fresh versus being packaged.

00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:08.850
You know what I mean by that, and

00:04:08.850 --> 00:04:11.280
we can tell the difference
when things are inauthentic.

00:04:13.010 --> 00:04:16.530
One of my favorite alums, Leo Lindbeck
always said, think of the admission

00:04:16.530 --> 00:04:20.160
process not as a marketing activity where
you're trying to look at someone and

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:23.735
deliver exactly what they want, But
to look at it as an accounting activity,

00:04:23.735 --> 00:04:27.049
where you're looking inside and
then trying to express that clearly.

00:04:28.190 --> 00:04:32.700
I think that's a good shorthand for
me of what the aim is of the essays.

00:04:34.510 --> 00:04:38.510
I hope that candidates are reading
what we said on the website because

00:04:38.510 --> 00:04:40.890
we do encourage people to get feedback.

00:04:42.930 --> 00:04:48.390
It's helpful to get a sense from people
who know you very well on whether

00:04:48.390 --> 00:04:52.670
the essays are coming across
in your voice, whether this is

00:04:52.670 --> 00:04:57.450
the brother that I know or this is the
wife that I know coming through on paper.

00:04:57.450 --> 00:04:59.280
But I'd caution you to

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:03.360
think carefully about the difference
between feedback and coaching.

00:05:04.780 --> 00:05:07.650
&gt;&gt; Can you say a little bit more
about that, the difference?

00:05:07.650 --> 00:05:11.590
&gt;&gt; I think feedback is, you've,
you've done the reflection,

00:05:11.590 --> 00:05:14.030
you've done the thinking,
you've put in the effort and

00:05:14.030 --> 00:05:19.010
you're getting consanguinity checks from
people who really know you to ensure that

00:05:19.010 --> 00:05:22.750
what you're putting on paper is authentic,
but it does reflect you.

00:05:22.750 --> 00:05:23.830
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:05:23.830 --> 00:05:28.140
&gt;&gt; That's a couple times, that's one or
two times, that's not many,

00:05:28.140 --> 00:05:32.030
many hours of people giving you
wordsmithing, and rearranging

00:05:32.030 --> 00:05:37.640
your flow of your topics, and telling you
what Stanford wants, that kind of thing.

00:05:37.640 --> 00:05:40.790
&gt;&gt; Let's go on to letters
of recommendations.

00:05:40.790 --> 00:05:43.750
Again, all the requirements
are on our website,

00:05:43.750 --> 00:05:47.580
including the questions that we are asking
your recommenders, so of course,

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:49.580
you're going to want to
take a look at that.

00:05:49.580 --> 00:05:52.780
There are two letters of
reference that are required.

00:05:52.780 --> 00:05:58.964
The first one would come from a current,
direct supervisor at work or

00:05:58.964 --> 00:06:05.680
as we say, next best alternative,
there are certainly situations where

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:09.590
you may not be able to get a reference
from your current direct supervisor.

00:06:09.590 --> 00:06:13.680
You might be self -mployed or
working at a family business or

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:18.650
in a new position where your supervisor
really doesn't know you well yet, or

00:06:18.650 --> 00:06:22.180
maybe you can't even tell your
employer that you're applying.

00:06:22.180 --> 00:06:27.188
If you can't get one from your current
direct supervisor, what would be,

00:06:27.188 --> 00:06:30.470
Derrick, other alternatives?

00:06:30.470 --> 00:06:33.390
&gt;&gt; I think this is probably
the most commonly asked question at

00:06:33.390 --> 00:06:34.490
information sessions.

00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:38.740
The short answer is use your best
judgement because you're going to know far

00:06:38.740 --> 00:06:43.000
better than we who are good
choices to provide that insight.

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:45.990
But I would think, if you're working in
a services firm, it could be a client.

00:06:45.990 --> 00:06:50.340
If you work in a sales role,
it might be a customer, if you have

00:06:52.060 --> 00:06:57.510
extensive operations with management,
it could be a previous manager and

00:06:57.510 --> 00:07:00.730
direct supervisor, or
a portfolio company you've worked with.

00:07:00.730 --> 00:07:07.020
Someone you work with at your organization
who has direct exposure to you and who

00:07:07.020 --> 00:07:10.880
cares enough about you to make the time to
write a detailed and thoughtful letter.

00:07:12.070 --> 00:07:15.600
But I think you're trying to get,
as Allison said, the next best alternative

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:19.329
means as close to that current
direct supervisor as possible.

00:07:20.620 --> 00:07:25.620
&gt;&gt; The second reference actually
has a lot of flexibility for you.

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:29.990
You really have four different
choices here for the second reference.

00:07:29.990 --> 00:07:32.800
It could be again, another

00:07:35.380 --> 00:07:40.750
supervisor or a peer and
those could come from the workplace.

00:07:40.750 --> 00:07:46.630
Or it could be someone from outside
the workplace who's perhaps worked

00:07:46.630 --> 00:07:51.736
with your or supervised you in
an extracurricular activity.

00:07:51.736 --> 00:07:56.171
Those four quadrants are what
you have to choose from.

00:07:56.171 --> 00:07:58.924
In general though,
Derrick, would you say we,

00:07:58.924 --> 00:08:02.429
the strongest reference typically
come from the work place.

00:08:02.429 --> 00:08:07.530
&gt;&gt; Absolutely, I think, again,
with thousands of applicants,

00:08:07.530 --> 00:08:10.559
each person's situation is unique.

00:08:10.559 --> 00:08:15.070
But in most cases, what we've observed
is that the recommender who's

00:08:15.070 --> 00:08:19.728
worked with you for 40 or 50 hours
a week for six weeks, or six months,

00:08:19.728 --> 00:08:23.938
or three or four years,
has deeper insight into your strengths and

00:08:23.938 --> 00:08:29.051
your areas of development, than someone
who sees you in a volunteer activity for

00:08:29.051 --> 00:08:33.350
a few hours a week,
even if it's a sustained commitment.

00:08:33.350 --> 00:08:36.760
So, you just have to be careful
in thinking about those choices.

00:08:36.760 --> 00:08:40.590
We are kind of on your side as applicants
when we're reading the applications,

00:08:40.590 --> 00:08:42.730
so it's not that we're going to look and

00:08:42.730 --> 00:08:45.500
say this really doesn't give
us what we're looking for.

00:08:45.500 --> 00:08:49.600
But I do see files every year
where I read it and say gosh,

00:08:49.600 --> 00:08:51.420
the recommender really
let this person down.

00:08:52.510 --> 00:08:57.960
&gt;&gt; All right,
let's talk a bit about how somebody

00:08:57.960 --> 00:09:02.590
would go about choosing their
recommenders, advice about that, Derek.

00:09:03.880 --> 00:09:08.140
&gt;&gt; Sure, well, this brings me back to
my point earlier that both essays and

00:09:08.140 --> 00:09:09.409
recommendations are stories.

00:09:10.650 --> 00:09:12.530
Essays are stories that you're telling us,

00:09:12.530 --> 00:09:16.400
and recommendations are stories that
others are telling us about you.

00:09:16.400 --> 00:09:21.200
The reason that we ask for references
in addition to the essays is that

00:09:21.200 --> 00:09:24.550
others see things in you that
you may not see in yourself.

00:09:24.550 --> 00:09:27.990
And so it's really helpful for us to have
both those perspectives as we're trying to

00:09:27.990 --> 00:09:30.240
understand you a little bit better.

00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:32.840
You can see exactly what
we're asking the recommender.

00:09:32.840 --> 00:09:36.250
So, I strongly encourage anyone who's
applying to go to the letter of reference

00:09:36.250 --> 00:09:41.480
page on our website, to look at the
traits, the competencies that we're asking

00:09:41.480 --> 00:09:46.060
the recommenders to assess, as well as
the three questions we ask them to answer.

00:09:46.060 --> 00:09:51.130
But I suggest someone who can provide,
at a tactical level, detailed anecdotes,

00:09:51.130 --> 00:09:54.950
who can give examples, who's excited
to write a letter for you and

00:09:54.950 --> 00:09:59.070
will actually spend sufficient
time to write a thoughtful letter.

00:09:59.070 --> 00:10:04.310
At a more philosophical level,
pick someone who is

00:10:04.310 --> 00:10:09.300
in a position to see how you've developed,
who cares about your career,

00:10:09.300 --> 00:10:11.809
and who is rooting for you to succeed.

00:10:13.180 --> 00:10:15.830
&gt;&gt; I think sometimes,
as you're approaching a recommender,

00:10:15.830 --> 00:10:20.230
the conversations that
come out of even asking

00:10:20.230 --> 00:10:24.344
them to write a recommendation,
can really be constructive conversations.

00:10:25.505 --> 00:10:30.265
&gt;&gt; In a lot of ways, one of
the things you hear us say, Allison,

00:10:30.265 --> 00:10:31.555
you know this, but Candace may not.

00:10:31.555 --> 00:10:33.935
We often say that we're looking for
candidates for

00:10:33.935 --> 00:10:37.385
whom success has been
a byproduct rather than a goal.

00:10:37.385 --> 00:10:39.205
And that notion of byproduct and

00:10:39.205 --> 00:10:41.965
goal comes to mind as I think
about references as well.

00:10:41.965 --> 00:10:46.615
Because in a lot of ways, the goal of the
recommendation could be to have a great

00:10:46.615 --> 00:10:50.700
conversation with your recommender,
to talk about your experiences and

00:10:50.700 --> 00:10:54.240
to talk about things that you've done
well or areas that you may need to grow.

00:10:54.240 --> 00:10:57.630
And to get that person's feedback
on those experiences, and

00:10:57.630 --> 00:11:01.730
if that conversation is a productive
one for you and for the recommender,

00:11:01.730 --> 00:11:06.560
the byproduct of that is a great letter of
recommendation that he or she turns in.

00:11:06.560 --> 00:11:08.746
So, I think it's great to sit
down with your recommender,

00:11:08.746 --> 00:11:13.370
this is not an activity that
I'd say simply register him or

00:11:13.370 --> 00:11:16.850
her and then make sure the person
turns it in by the deadline.

00:11:16.850 --> 00:11:19.540
No, no, no, I think you actually
do want to invest a little more.

00:11:19.540 --> 00:11:22.360
Sit down over coffee,
or tea, or champagne,

00:11:22.360 --> 00:11:26.990
whatever the situation might be, and have
a good, kind of coaching conversation.

00:11:26.990 --> 00:11:31.339
And I think it can be beneficial, whether
or not you even apply to business school.

00:11:33.510 --> 00:11:38.360
&gt;&gt; What about,
we often hear applicants worry about

00:11:38.360 --> 00:11:42.360
if they approach the recommender at work,
that sometimes the recommender will say,

00:11:42.360 --> 00:11:46.360
well, why don't you draft it for
me, and I'll sign it?

00:11:46.360 --> 00:11:48.450
What should people do in that situation?

00:11:48.450 --> 00:11:51.220
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and
I know that's a tough situation, actually.

00:11:51.220 --> 00:11:54.224
When I was applying to business
school last millennium,

00:11:54.224 --> 00:11:58.354
I had a manager who felt strongly that
every consultant in the firm who asked him

00:11:58.354 --> 00:12:01.990
to write a recommendation should
draft it on his or her own.

00:12:01.990 --> 00:12:04.780
So I ultimately ended up
picking someone else.

00:12:04.780 --> 00:12:09.658
The goal here, I'll reiterate, is to get
insights about you from someone else, and

00:12:09.658 --> 00:12:14.230
if you were drafting the recommendation,
then I think it defeats the purpose.

00:12:14.230 --> 00:12:18.921
You end up kind of doubling down on
the things that you already know

00:12:18.921 --> 00:12:23.530
about yourself, whereas
the recommenders should be providing

00:12:23.530 --> 00:12:27.901
insights that may be new to you and
hopefully may be new to us.

00:12:27.901 --> 00:12:28.840
Yeah, that's it.

00:12:28.840 --> 00:12:33.680
&gt;&gt; Okay, let's move on to talking
about some of the questions that you,

00:12:33.680 --> 00:12:38.290
as prospective applicants,
might be asking yourselves as you

00:12:38.290 --> 00:12:42.330
get ready to write your essays and
approach those recommenders.

00:12:42.330 --> 00:12:44.400
You will find that answering
these questions for

00:12:44.400 --> 00:12:49.300
yourself now could really save you some
time later in the application process.

00:12:49.300 --> 00:12:54.850
So, one question you should be asking
yourself is, do I actually have time

00:12:54.850 --> 00:13:00.100
to do all that thinking and reflecting and
self-examining before I write my essays?

00:13:00.100 --> 00:13:02.315
&gt;&gt; Yeah, but
I think there's no shortcut here.

00:13:02.315 --> 00:13:09.160
We're looking for a holistic view of you,
the applicant, as a person, your values,

00:13:09.160 --> 00:13:15.020
your passions, your ideals, your
experiences, your hopes, and your dreams.

00:13:15.020 --> 00:13:17.940
And our goal is to understand
what motivates you and

00:13:17.940 --> 00:13:20.730
how you've become the person that
you are today, and then, again,

00:13:20.730 --> 00:13:23.700
how Stanford can help you in terms
of realizing your ambitions.

00:13:23.700 --> 00:13:27.440
And so I think you just have to
be prepared to think a lot and

00:13:27.440 --> 00:13:30.750
then write, and for each person,
it will be different.

00:13:30.750 --> 00:13:31.340
For one person,

00:13:31.340 --> 00:13:35.540
it might be a morning run where you kind
of find some time to clear your head.

00:13:35.540 --> 00:13:39.190
For another person, it might be the subway
ride to and from the office, but

00:13:39.190 --> 00:13:42.540
there's no shortcut here, so you really
do have to put in that time to think.

00:13:44.140 --> 00:13:44.690
&gt;&gt; So Derrick,

00:13:44.690 --> 00:13:48.890
you had talked about how the essays and
recommendations are stories.

00:13:48.890 --> 00:13:52.450
So another question people
should ask themselves is,

00:13:52.450 --> 00:13:55.565
what is a story that only I can tell?

00:13:55.565 --> 00:14:00.420
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm, and I think that what makes
the story that only you can tell

00:14:00.420 --> 00:14:04.870
is not the person, or the event, or
the situation that you're talking about.

00:14:04.870 --> 00:14:07.480
But how that has affected you and

00:14:07.480 --> 00:14:11.550
how that's shaped your outlook,
your perspective, your choices.

00:14:11.550 --> 00:14:15.210
So I'll reiterate what I say on
the road all the time, which is,

00:14:15.210 --> 00:14:17.360
don't worry about standing out.

00:14:17.360 --> 00:14:19.790
You never know what other
applicants are sharing with us.

00:14:19.790 --> 00:14:24.390
I think differentiation occurs naturally,
if you concentrate on telling us who you

00:14:24.390 --> 00:14:27.340
are, and
no one can know that better than you.

00:14:27.340 --> 00:14:29.410
What I see happen all
the time with applicants,

00:14:29.410 --> 00:14:35.300
especially applicants
who have the fortune or

00:14:35.300 --> 00:14:40.240
misfortune of being in large communities,
where there are many people applying,

00:14:40.240 --> 00:14:43.420
is that they will lose faith in
themselves throughout the process.

00:14:43.420 --> 00:14:48.940
And so start comparing, my GPA is
two-tenths of a point lower than hers,

00:14:48.940 --> 00:14:51.570
and he was promoted three
months ahead of me.

00:14:51.570 --> 00:14:54.640
And it's easy to get focused
on all the little aspects,

00:14:54.640 --> 00:14:57.150
where someone may have
done better than you.

00:14:57.150 --> 00:15:00.170
But we're reading the application,
looking for strengths, and so

00:15:00.170 --> 00:15:03.610
I just want to reiterate that we
want candidates to really have faith

00:15:03.610 --> 00:15:06.660
in yourselves, have some confidence
as you go through the process.

00:15:06.660 --> 00:15:08.870
Think about the essays as
conversations on paper.

00:15:10.120 --> 00:15:12.720
&gt;&gt; Well, have confidence and
faith in yourself, and

00:15:12.720 --> 00:15:14.900
don't compare yourself to other people.

00:15:14.900 --> 00:15:18.501
That's good advice not only for writing
applications, [LAUGH] but good advice for

00:15:18.501 --> 00:15:20.150
life, I think.

00:15:20.150 --> 00:15:22.940
&gt;&gt; Honestly, I think the application
process, if done correctly,

00:15:22.940 --> 00:15:27.080
is great in terms of pulling
candidates up from working so

00:15:27.080 --> 00:15:29.910
hard on their daily goals and
asking some more fundamental

00:15:29.910 --> 00:15:33.690
questions that'll be helpful in whether or
not they apply to business school.

00:15:33.690 --> 00:15:35.630
&gt;&gt; So,
another question to ask yourself is,

00:15:35.630 --> 00:15:39.085
how will Stanford help me turn
my dreams into a reality?

00:15:39.085 --> 00:15:43.030
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm, well, I guess the key there is,
do you have some dreams?

00:15:43.030 --> 00:15:45.799
Do you have some sense of focused
interest of what you want to do after

00:15:45.799 --> 00:15:46.594
business school?

00:15:46.594 --> 00:15:49.393
I think Stanford's a really magical place,
and

00:15:49.393 --> 00:15:53.147
if you come here with something in mind,
you can take advantage of

00:15:53.147 --> 00:15:57.586
all the resources at the university,
from business school to engineering,

00:15:57.586 --> 00:16:01.708
or law, medical school, whatever it is,
to make your dreams happen.

00:16:01.708 --> 00:16:06.391
But, have some ideas of what you best
self would be after Stanford and

00:16:06.391 --> 00:16:09.150
related goals for your education here.

00:16:09.150 --> 00:16:10.680
You don't need to make them up,
by the way.

00:16:10.680 --> 00:16:14.810
If you don't have really specific goals,
there's a reason that we

00:16:14.810 --> 00:16:19.430
can put the goals themselves as kind of
a tweet, a text box in the application.

00:16:19.430 --> 00:16:23.070
Whereas the essay is focussed on how
you're going to maximise your experience

00:16:23.070 --> 00:16:29.675
and make the most of
your time at Stanford.

00:16:29.675 --> 00:16:34.880
&gt;&gt; Okay, well let's move on now
to questions that the applicants,

00:16:34.880 --> 00:16:37.410
I'm sorry, that the audience,
we hope you'll be applicants, but

00:16:37.410 --> 00:16:40.950
that the audience have
submitted in advance.

00:16:42.520 --> 00:16:47.010
What kinds of information should
I be providing to my recommender?

00:16:47.010 --> 00:16:52.940
&gt;&gt; I think that's going to depend on
your relationship with your recommender.

00:16:52.940 --> 00:16:56.700
I've seen candidates who provide
the entire application and

00:16:56.700 --> 00:16:57.930
essay to the recommender.

00:16:57.930 --> 00:17:00.190
And I've seen candidates who
simply sit down with him or

00:17:00.190 --> 00:17:03.810
her and talk through the process.

00:17:05.180 --> 00:17:08.760
You don't have to worry that every single

00:17:08.760 --> 00:17:12.870
part of your application makes it look
like you've been lined up since birth

00:17:12.870 --> 00:17:15.670
to be admitted to business
school at Stanford, right?

00:17:15.670 --> 00:17:18.090
Life is messy, we understand that.

00:17:18.090 --> 00:17:20.630
Not all of your choices will make
sense as you're making them.

00:17:20.630 --> 00:17:23.860
But our hope is, again, that when
you look back on them, you're able

00:17:23.860 --> 00:17:28.920
to extract some common themes and
kind of connect the dots as you look back.

00:17:28.920 --> 00:17:32.960
But again, it's not like you have to
ensure that every single thing that

00:17:32.960 --> 00:17:36.800
your recommender says is perfectly aligned
with every single thing that you say.

00:17:38.040 --> 00:17:42.190
So I don't think you have to go
overboard on providing information, but

00:17:42.190 --> 00:17:44.450
again I'll reiterate,
there's benefit to you,

00:17:44.450 --> 00:17:48.020
as an applicant, from sitting down and
talking with your recommender,

00:17:48.020 --> 00:17:51.060
who is a person who knows you well,
a person who cares about you and

00:17:51.060 --> 00:17:54.680
who's opinion you value, about your
performance and about your future.

00:17:56.590 --> 00:18:00.730
&gt;&gt; Is it ever worth it to submit an
additional letter of recommendation beyond

00:18:00.730 --> 00:18:02.369
the two that we asked for?

00:18:03.700 --> 00:18:08.240
Yeah, well, Allison, you've been through
our admission committee discussions many,

00:18:08.240 --> 00:18:12.820
many times as we think about what
the requirements are for an application.

00:18:12.820 --> 00:18:16.680
So we have asked for
two recommendations because we think

00:18:16.680 --> 00:18:19.250
that is the most helpful
across our applicant pool.

00:18:20.250 --> 00:18:25.250
We do, however, give candidates a bit of
flexibility on what they're providing, but

00:18:25.250 --> 00:18:26.680
it's a risky move.

00:18:26.680 --> 00:18:31.300
Right, so, if you, for example, give us
a third recommendation, first of all,

00:18:31.300 --> 00:18:35.300
I can't guarantee that we'll read it,
because it really depends on when it gets

00:18:35.300 --> 00:18:39.320
submitted, but let's say it was submitted
with your application at the time

00:18:39.320 --> 00:18:43.108
you applied and we read that, well, the
question from the reader is going to be,

00:18:43.108 --> 00:18:47.430
this is a helpful recommendation or
an unhelpful recommendation?

00:18:47.430 --> 00:18:49.750
If its helpful, then i'm going to say,

00:18:49.750 --> 00:18:52.890
why was it not one of the two
other recommendations.

00:18:52.890 --> 00:18:55.980
If it's unhelpful,
I may ask why is it submitted at all?

00:18:55.980 --> 00:19:01.450
It's not a risk free move, because it does
call into question the self awareness and

00:19:01.450 --> 00:19:02.830
judgment of the candidate.

00:19:02.830 --> 00:19:05.400
And frankly,
there's also an issue around fairness.

00:19:05.400 --> 00:19:09.950
I kind of think of the admission process,
it's almost like when you take a test,

00:19:09.950 --> 00:19:14.110
you turn the test in at a certain point,
and then it gets graded, and

00:19:14.110 --> 00:19:16.490
then we set the curve, and
then you get the grades back.

00:19:16.490 --> 00:19:21.830
Well, providing extra letters of reference
is, in a way, like giving someone a chance

00:19:21.830 --> 00:19:27.160
to do extra work on a test or to change
the answers after it's been turned in.

00:19:27.160 --> 00:19:29.370
That's not exactly fair to
all the other candidates.

00:19:29.370 --> 00:19:33.430
So, we'll always try to get
applicants flexibility, but

00:19:33.430 --> 00:19:36.920
I do think that they have to
think very carefully about it.

00:19:36.920 --> 00:19:41.530
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and I should add that it will
certainly be added to your application

00:19:41.530 --> 00:19:43.060
in terms of what do we do with it.

00:19:43.060 --> 00:19:46.470
But, as Derrick said,
it may not be read depending on

00:19:46.470 --> 00:19:50.320
if we've already read your application,
or where we are in the process.

00:19:50.320 --> 00:19:51.930
&gt;&gt; Again, it's like taking a test.

00:19:51.930 --> 00:19:52.920
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
You turn it in.

00:19:52.920 --> 00:19:56.627
&gt;&gt; Once the first persons grade your test,
we're not going to keep adding and

00:19:56.627 --> 00:19:59.140
letting you change
the answers on it after that.

00:19:59.140 --> 00:20:00.850
&gt;&gt; Right.
We talked about how the second

00:20:00.850 --> 00:20:06.530
recommendation can come from a supervisor,
or next best alternative, or a peer.

00:20:06.530 --> 00:20:09.270
So, Derrick,
can you talk a little bit about what

00:20:09.270 --> 00:20:13.850
counts as a good recommendation from a
peer, as opposed to one from a supervisor?

00:20:13.850 --> 00:20:16.065
And, again,
we're talking about that second reference.

00:20:16.065 --> 00:20:17.290
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:20:17.290 --> 00:20:20.750
The reason that we think a peer
can be helpful in the first place

00:20:20.750 --> 00:20:24.170
is that most of you will spend
your careers after business school

00:20:25.630 --> 00:20:29.635
leading without authority,
in a lot of cases.

00:20:29.635 --> 00:20:33.030
You're always, basically, trying to move
the organization, or your department, or

00:20:33.030 --> 00:20:36.730
your team, or whatever it is,
probably a level ahead of whatever your

00:20:36.730 --> 00:20:40.120
actual job responsibilities are,
whatever your actual title is.

00:20:40.120 --> 00:20:43.870
And so, peers are folks who
influence without authority, right?

00:20:43.870 --> 00:20:48.047
And so, someone who can give us insight
on what it's like to work with you very

00:20:48.047 --> 00:20:52.620
closely, elbow to elbow, shoulder
to shoulder, can be really valuable

00:20:52.620 --> 00:20:56.280
in understanding how you'll participate
as a member of our community,

00:20:56.280 --> 00:21:01.990
which is a very strong, pure learning
community, as well as giving us a sense of

00:21:01.990 --> 00:21:05.000
areas where you'll want to
develop as a student and

00:21:05.000 --> 00:21:08.700
strengths that you'll draw upon
once you're an alumnus or alumna.

00:21:08.700 --> 00:21:11.450
&gt;&gt; Okay, here's another question.

00:21:11.450 --> 00:21:15.050
Both of my supervisors speak
English as a second language.

00:21:15.050 --> 00:21:19.080
If they're not able to write as
eloquently as a native English speaker,

00:21:19.080 --> 00:21:20.955
is that going to affect my candidacy?

00:21:20.955 --> 00:21:22.300
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:21:22.300 --> 00:21:24.470
And, short answer would be no.

00:21:24.470 --> 00:21:28.410
We are, with apologies to vegans,
looking for a steak, not the sizzle.

00:21:29.494 --> 00:21:34.713
If it's a real

00:21:37.438 --> 00:21:40.243
drawback for the recommender, then he or

00:21:40.243 --> 00:21:44.960
she could submit the recommendation and
have it translated.

00:21:44.960 --> 00:21:47.670
And we just would ask for
the original as well as the translation.

00:21:47.670 --> 00:21:50.780
We don't want the translation done
by the applicant, by the way.

00:21:50.780 --> 00:21:53.990
So if someone else was in a position to
do that translation, then we'd ask for

00:21:53.990 --> 00:21:56.880
that persons contact information as well.

00:21:56.880 --> 00:22:01.800
But no, we are able to extract from
recommendations the basic premise

00:22:01.800 --> 00:22:05.440
that's coming through the recommendation,
how the applicant has performed and

00:22:05.440 --> 00:22:07.580
what makes him or
her successful in a role.

00:22:07.580 --> 00:22:12.530
It really won't come down to word choice
or things that are particular nuanced,

00:22:12.530 --> 00:22:13.630
is my sense.

00:22:13.630 --> 00:22:15.160
&gt;&gt; Okay, so it's really the content.

00:22:15.160 --> 00:22:16.730
It's not the syntax, or the, yeah.

00:22:16.730 --> 00:22:18.000
&gt;&gt; It's the gist of it.

00:22:18.000 --> 00:22:20.350
&gt;&gt; Right.
&gt;&gt; The.

00:22:20.350 --> 00:22:22.780
&gt;&gt; Derrick, what's your opinion of

00:22:22.780 --> 00:22:26.430
using an academic reference
versus a professional reference?

00:22:27.580 --> 00:22:30.210
&gt;&gt; When we think about references for
Stanford,

00:22:30.210 --> 00:22:33.350
we're looking at them
as a way to understand

00:22:33.350 --> 00:22:37.260
ways that you've demonstrated leadership
and understand your leadership potential.

00:22:37.260 --> 00:22:42.240
And so, in most cases, academic references
have not had the opportunity to

00:22:42.240 --> 00:22:45.010
see candidates demonstrate leadership.

00:22:46.890 --> 00:22:48.740
Again, there are obviously exceptions.

00:22:48.740 --> 00:22:51.010
Maybe you've been a teaching assistant for
a professor.

00:22:51.010 --> 00:22:53.002
Maybe you've done a research project for
him or her.

00:22:53.002 --> 00:22:58.370
And so, that faculty member would
be able to provide some detail,

00:22:58.370 --> 00:22:59.630
anecdotes, or evidence.

00:22:59.630 --> 00:23:02.380
But for the most part,
when we see academic references,

00:23:02.380 --> 00:23:07.310
they tend to be very brief,
they tend to be generic and sad to say,

00:23:07.310 --> 00:23:10.530
in a lot of cases, they talk about the
faculty member than about the candidate.

00:23:10.530 --> 00:23:15.320
If you're a college senior and
you're applying for a deferred admission,

00:23:16.380 --> 00:23:19.050
then the academic reference may be
the only choice that you have, so

00:23:19.050 --> 00:23:23.840
we see them more frequently for folks
who are applying for deferred enrolment.

00:23:23.840 --> 00:23:28.040
And in those cases, obviously, we know how
to calibrate them, but just be careful.

00:23:28.040 --> 00:23:31.855
This applies to anybody but be thoughtful
and careful as you're thinking about your

00:23:31.855 --> 00:23:37.020
reccommender to ensure that here she
is able to address the specific traits,

00:23:37.020 --> 00:23:41.740
the competencies, the characteristics that
we're asking on the recommendation form,

00:23:41.740 --> 00:23:42.620
and in the questions.

00:23:42.620 --> 00:23:43.211
And, again,

00:23:43.211 --> 00:23:46.575
I'll suggest looking at those on the
website before picking your recommender.

00:23:46.575 --> 00:23:51.459
Because if we get a form that says no
basis for judgement for 85% of the things

00:23:51.459 --> 00:23:56.423
that we are asking, well, that doesn't
help you and it also doesn't help us.

00:23:56.423 --> 00:23:59.280
&gt;&gt; Okay, how about for reapplicants?

00:23:59.280 --> 00:24:04.344
What's the expectation for
letters of rec and essays?

00:24:04.344 --> 00:24:07.968
I mean, is it going to seem weird
that what matters most to you,

00:24:07.968 --> 00:24:12.751
which is such a fundamental topic changes
significantly from the year before?

00:24:14.590 --> 00:24:19.494
&gt;&gt; Our view has been that as a
reapplicant, you're evaluated on the basis

00:24:19.494 --> 00:24:24.005
of your new application in the context
of the new applicant pool.

00:24:24.005 --> 00:24:28.347
There are sister institutions of ours
that will say, you applied before.

00:24:28.347 --> 00:24:32.596
Tell us what's changed since the last
application and we don't do that.

00:24:32.596 --> 00:24:37.225
It always felt to me that asking that
question came from an understanding that

00:24:37.225 --> 00:24:39.086
my last decision was correct.

00:24:39.086 --> 00:24:43.653
So, please explain to me what's changed
to change my decision and the realty at

00:24:43.653 --> 00:24:48.564
Stanford is that there's so many qualified
candidates that we're simply not able to

00:24:48.564 --> 00:24:52.960
admit all of the wonderful people
we'd like to admit each year.

00:24:52.960 --> 00:24:57.770
So I don't ever assume that simply because
someone applied was admitted that,

00:24:57.770 --> 00:25:02.966
that was the correct decision, but there
was something a mess with the application.

00:25:02.966 --> 00:25:07.807
All that to say, you can turn in the exact
same application in two different years.

00:25:07.807 --> 00:25:10.523
And I think you could get different
results not because you've changed, but

00:25:10.523 --> 00:25:12.600
because the applicant pool has changed.

00:25:12.600 --> 00:25:16.819
But it's what matters most to you and
why it has changed dramatically over

00:25:16.819 --> 00:25:21.461
the course of, in previous eight or
12 months, then the topic should change.

00:25:21.461 --> 00:25:25.130
And if it hasn't changed, then it
should remain similar to what it was.

00:25:25.130 --> 00:25:29.310
But I would definitely take advantage
of the opportunity to provide updated

00:25:29.310 --> 00:25:34.076
references, because my assumption is most
of our candidates, most of you are getting

00:25:34.076 --> 00:25:38.584
stronger with time as you are building
experience and exposure and so recommender

00:25:38.584 --> 00:25:43.056
today would be better than a recommender
who had exposure to you 12 months ago.

00:25:43.056 --> 00:25:45.621
So, just making sure that
you take advantage of that.

00:25:45.621 --> 00:25:50.791
&gt;&gt; I would add that even if the topic
is the same, probably your perspective

00:25:50.791 --> 00:25:56.890
on it has changed with another year or
two of life and working experience.

00:25:56.890 --> 00:26:01.841
&gt;&gt; And as the coda, I will say that we do
not compare your previous application to

00:26:01.841 --> 00:26:03.681
your current application.

00:26:03.681 --> 00:26:07.714
So as the assistant dean of admission,
I would look at both applications

00:26:07.714 --> 00:26:10.833
at the point that the file
gets back from the interview.

00:26:10.833 --> 00:26:13.643
And that's not looking at
it to compare word for

00:26:13.643 --> 00:26:18.635
word what was this application versus the
last one, but looking was there some major

00:26:18.635 --> 00:26:22.594
red flag that we saw the last time and
wasn't addressed this time.

00:26:22.594 --> 00:26:26.422
But for the most part, it's not going to
make a difference in my decision and

00:26:26.422 --> 00:26:30.622
I would be the only person in the entire
admissions office who would have access to

00:26:30.622 --> 00:26:31.893
the old applications.

00:26:31.893 --> 00:26:37.838
So, it really is a new application on its
own merit in the context of the new pool.

00:26:37.838 --> 00:26:42.749
&gt;&gt; How about client recommendations for
people in client service businesses?

00:26:42.749 --> 00:26:44.681
Are there common mistakes or

00:26:44.681 --> 00:26:49.842
are there ways that they can be enhanced
that applicants should be aware of?

00:26:49.842 --> 00:26:51.653
&gt;&gt; I think it kind of
depends on the client.

00:26:51.653 --> 00:26:55.898
It depends on how familiar he or she is
with recommendations to graduate school

00:26:55.898 --> 00:26:58.167
and with business school in particular.

00:26:58.167 --> 00:27:04.319
So we have on our website, a podcast,
I think and definitely a transcript of

00:27:04.319 --> 00:27:09.719
the podcast where we talk about
what makes a recommendation sing.

00:27:09.719 --> 00:27:11.832
Like what really brings
a candidate to life?

00:27:11.832 --> 00:27:17.326
And so if you're asking a client who this
may be the first recommendation ever,

00:27:17.326 --> 00:27:21.677
then you might want him or
her to take a look at that transcript.

00:27:21.677 --> 00:27:25.159
I think it would be helpful, but
I was just reiterate to the client,

00:27:25.159 --> 00:27:27.308
just as I would reiterate to anyone else.

00:27:27.308 --> 00:27:30.655
How important the recommendation
is in the process and

00:27:30.655 --> 00:27:35.470
I would be really grateful to him or her
for making the time to write the letter.

00:27:35.470 --> 00:27:39.857
&gt;&gt; There's so many good questions here,
but I think we really only have time for

00:27:39.857 --> 00:27:40.473
one more.

00:27:40.473 --> 00:27:44.470
Should the letters of recommendation and
essays compliment each other or

00:27:44.470 --> 00:27:47.834
should they emphasis different
things about the candidate?

00:27:47.834 --> 00:27:49.587
&gt;&gt; I don't say, there's no should.

00:27:49.587 --> 00:27:54.328
Sometimes, we see letters of reference and

00:27:54.328 --> 00:27:57.668
essays that are reinforcing.

00:27:57.668 --> 00:28:00.999
Sometimes, we see where those
provide different insight.

00:28:00.999 --> 00:28:04.931
Sometimes they help us reinterpret
things we've seen, but

00:28:04.931 --> 00:28:07.244
it's always helpful in context and

00:28:07.244 --> 00:28:12.424
as you're looking at the applicant
holistically to have those perspectives.

00:28:12.424 --> 00:28:14.293
&gt;&gt; Well, we're almost out of time.

00:28:14.293 --> 00:28:17.078
If you didn't get your
question answered today,

00:28:17.078 --> 00:28:21.900
please feel free to contact our office
by email or phone to ask your question.

00:28:21.900 --> 00:28:24.606
&gt;&gt; Before we go, Derrick,
any words of advice for

00:28:24.606 --> 00:28:28.343
our listeners today in terms of
the letters of rec and the essays?

00:28:28.343 --> 00:28:30.400
&gt;&gt; Gosh, well, first, I'd say,

00:28:30.400 --> 00:28:35.181
you're putting yourself in a great
position by tuning in for the call today.

00:28:35.181 --> 00:28:39.770
We have information sessions on campus
here at Stanford on typically Mondays and

00:28:39.770 --> 00:28:40.382
Fridays.

00:28:40.382 --> 00:28:43.145
We have off-campus sessions
happening all around the world.

00:28:43.145 --> 00:28:46.633
I'm literally flying out in about
30 minutes for New York where we'll

00:28:46.633 --> 00:28:50.135
be hosting sessions later this week and
then I'll be in India next week.

00:28:50.135 --> 00:28:53.591
So we have sessions happening all
around the world and our goal in

00:28:53.591 --> 00:28:57.762
traveling to where you are, as applicants
is to be able to answer questions.

00:28:57.762 --> 00:29:01.901
So feel free to sign up for those and try
to come and meet some of our alumni and

00:29:01.901 --> 00:29:06.630
students, and learn more about
the program, and about the process.

00:29:06.630 --> 00:29:12.058
I'll reiterate that comparing
yourselves to each other can be bad for

00:29:12.058 --> 00:29:15.557
your morale,
as you go through the process.

00:29:15.557 --> 00:29:18.974
We really do look at every
candidate asking yourselves,

00:29:18.974 --> 00:29:21.548
what are the reasons to admit this person?

00:29:21.548 --> 00:29:23.800
So, it really is focused
on your strengths.

00:29:23.800 --> 00:29:28.493
And so, I'll reiterate to you as
candidates who cares about the things that

00:29:28.493 --> 00:29:30.047
